#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175803 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "8c4777a6-d831-54cc-8677-b05ea45b2b4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-7324" :question/problem "The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with a particular virus  follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} =  4P - 0.000\\,01  P^2,  \\qquad P(0) = 5\\,000,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the virus outbreak began. For which value of $P$ is the number of people infected with the virus growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\nP' = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P = \\dfrac12a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.000\\,01{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.000\\,01P(400\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 400\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $400\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac12a = \\dfrac12\\cdot 400\\,000 = 200\\,000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "6f96059e-ce3d-5771-b3d8-15044cfd5495" :question/math-academy-id "e-9375" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of some foxes in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\ny' =0.5y - 0.002y^2, \\qquad y(0) = 25,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible fox population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.5y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(250-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 250.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $250,$ and we conclude that the number of foxes cannot exceed $250.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/id #uuid "2096869c-2a41-51a5-b2e8-c1039af4d640" :question/math-academy-id "e-9376" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny' = 2y\\left(60 - \\frac{y}{3} \\right), \\qquad y(0) = 10\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)}?$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\ny' = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y(60-\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\  & =2y(\\frac{180-y}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{3}(180-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a = 180.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $180.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}{y(t)} = 180.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "068cdd7b-3012-5358-92a7-0c68f1e76683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174095} {:answer/id #uuid "0c574a47-936a-54b9-ac71-506ab0854566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174096} {:answer/id #uuid "1034f4af-c292-5fb4-9a85-a771a3a892c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174097} {:answer/id #uuid "55deb9a5-bf93-57d7-9493-8d13c13d6e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174098} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9155d4-a4cc-59e5-bd9f-c69415302b71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174099}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d04916e-2a37-50d5-b3db-87246180c681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174094}] :question/id #uuid "82fbd2ba-8dc6-52ea-8a84-7a3c5cc31fc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118557" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of turtles on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n$y^{′} = 1.5y - 0.001y^{2}, y(0) = 500$,\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of turtles?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176021 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91856ca3-4d9c-51c4-910b-27acba52156e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e648c9a-926a-5f59-9853-589fa9d9d8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250809} {:answer/id #uuid "736ac0bf-0dbe-5e68-9494-dc20cab9dc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251504} {:answer/id #uuid "99f6f0f3-641e-5e3c-9f53-a5dd99a5934a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251876} {:answer/id #uuid "c21bdf6c-9a6b-59cc-a86d-935920b73c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252266} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d8d39e-d9ee-5f7d-b8f8-1a4ec2e6d553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c21bdf6c-9a6b-59cc-a86d-935920b73c85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252266} :answer-field/id #uuid "52cd9670-efaa-5997-a26c-de39051a6431" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edf79f32-6c49-50fc-bc9d-59df10630ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-118581" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=720y-8{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=5\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y(90-y),\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=90.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $90.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40813bed-c283-5998-b908-85358de0d46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251091} {:answer/id #uuid "a4190740-8ee6-56ce-b602-223c593c07ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251968} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b0a46a-f606-5c39-a1c6-f00d88e31929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251981} {:answer/id #uuid "c93f40e7-f250-55a6-84c1-de5f6d5ba56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252332} {:answer/id #uuid "e94476cf-4519-5af3-b99e-898e3687a889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40813bed-c283-5998-b908-85358de0d46e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251091} :answer-field/id #uuid "da13ec6c-83c7-50f9-876b-4ab3d2ef21e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "819cfa7f-e175-52e2-b6a4-00c7d6cd6241" :question/math-academy-id "q-119849" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad1e903d-6456-5955-bf64-5759544d0d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174593} {:answer/id #uuid "1944f8c9-05ff-5c57-a35b-aa0e3f0427e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174594} {:answer/id #uuid "20245535-dd55-5be9-ada3-91a1d613aa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174595} {:answer/id #uuid "423cc1a6-073a-5e09-aa03-96abfe59bc2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174596} {:answer/id #uuid "5abf98f0-b7c2-5755-b326-ff12e8b19358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174597}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab0258f-7c09-5528-925a-c8ae2bfa6440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174592}] :question/id #uuid "3e06f869-86f4-5afe-a28b-daef542c7fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-15486" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$ of squirrels in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.0005P(6000 - P), P(0) = 1000$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the squirrel population growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8351bd80-e518-5ece-9f96-abee1eb4c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8351bd80-e518-5ece-9f96-abee1eb4c668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319789} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b990bb7-090c-58a0-b6cc-f6b6c5614b8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "542b4bd1-a5e6-5564-a6fe-12f10ea4329d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353483" :question/problem "The download count $P(t)$ of a viral mobile game follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P(8\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=600.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the download count growing at the fastest possible rate?\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/90/9019b26b417c757bbfab79afb39379c1fc7a892bc44e934c6f23c339deacfd68.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=8000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $8000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8000=4000.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175957 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7293561d-2ddf-581d-b086-9754fdb2ff16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Point of Fastest Increase of a Logistic Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "101ea620-7c24-59db-9258-511ec049dc5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330089} {:answer/id #uuid "255d3436-78af-5f95-8551-acbf5c7be1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330271} {:answer/id #uuid "605d8033-5655-5ed0-906f-5023ddbf89d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330804} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cc0c21-5861-5a8b-a662-8d40db92437b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,400" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331587} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0152f8-d567-5785-9344-68fb05ce830a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca0152f8-d567-5785-9344-68fb05ce830a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331739} :answer-field/id #uuid "1706aa51-cd5b-516d-bad0-ce97d9d20daa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7dc96cac-3ced-5c6f-a9e4-3db7edb477b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-51379" :question/problem "The population $P(t)$of rabbits in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the rabbit population growing at the fastest possible rate?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\,000=1\\,000.\n$$"}]}
